This directory is intentionally kept even though it is empty.

The Dockerfile copies /app/public into the runtime image, and that COPY fails
outright if the directory does not exist. It was removed along with the rest
of the create-next-app leftovers, which quietly broke `docker compose build`
for anyone following the Docker install path in the README.

Keeping the directory is the lower-surprise fix: the day someone adds a
favicon or a robots.txt it will simply work, rather than being silently
dropped from the image.
